  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Building Resilient Community Ecosystems in the Fight Against Child Labour</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Planting Naturals and its subsidiary Goldtree are committed to proving that organic palm oil can be produced responsibly while improving the lives of smallholder farmers. In Kailahun, Sierra Leone, poverty, food insecurity, and lack of schooling mean families often rely on children for hazardous farm work despite strict company policies prohibiting child labour.The project is set out to tackle these root caused by strengthening livelihoods, reducing household vulnerability, and building community systems that protect children while supporting a resilient and ethical supply chain.Key interventions included the establishment of community-based organisations to manage revolving loan schemes and seedbanks, helping households escape exploitative debt and invest in productive, child-labour-free livelihoods. Women were central to the approach, with thousands engaged in vegetable gardening, small-scale enterprises, and apprenticeships, supported by literacy and business training. These measures improved household income, strengthened food security, and increased women&apos;s decision-making power.To eliminate reliance on children&apos;s labour during harvests, 55 trained adult labour groups were introduced, providing safe and affordable services to farming households. Parallel efforts in education &#x2013; such as school monitoring committees, by-laws on attendance, and community volunteers &#x2013; ensured 98% regular attendance among children. These measures created a cultural shift in how communities value education over child work. As a result, household food insecurity dropped from 97% at baseline to under 2%, while hazardous child labour declined from 98.9% of households to just 5% by the project&apos;s end.The project also embedded child labour due diligence directly into supply chain operations. Company policies were updated, staff were trained, and regular monitoring systems were established to ensure compliance. These measures improved transparency, strengthened relationships with smallholders, and aligned operations with international standards. Beyond reducing social risks, the approach generated clear business value by fostering farmer loyalty, improving yields, and creating a more resilient and traceable supply chain.</narrative>
  </description>
